---
const base = `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')}/`;
---

<div class="lesson-content setup-guide">
  <p>
    Complete this preparation step before starting the SupportHub feature stack.
    Create your own repository from the prepared template; do not make changes in
    this workshop website repository or in the completed reference repository.
  </p>

  <section aria-labelledby="create-repository">
    <h2 id="create-repository">Create your workshop repository</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Open <a href="https://github.com/sinannar/agenticengineeringday2026-template">agenticengineeringday2026-template</a>.</li>
      <li>Open <strong>Use this template</strong> and choose <strong>Create a new repository</strong>.</li>
      <li>Choose a repository name, then create the repository under your GitHub account.</li>
      <li>Open your new repository and use its <strong>Code</strong> menu to create a Codespace.</li>
    </ol>
    <figure>
      <img
        src={`${base}images/workshop-setup/template-use.png`}
        alt="The template repository Use this template menu with Create a new repository highlighted."
      />
      <figcaption>Start from the prepared Aspire template by choosing <strong>Use this template → Create a new repository</strong>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <img
        src={`${base}images/workshop-setup/template-create-repository.png`}
        alt="GitHub Create a new repository page showing the selected template and repository name field."
      />
      <figcaption>Choose a name for your workshop repository and create it under your account.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="create-codespace">
    <h2 id="create-codespace">Create a Codespace</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>In your new repository, open <strong>Code</strong>, switch to <strong>Codespaces</strong>, and choose <strong>New with options…</strong>.</li>
      <li>Create the Codespace from your repository's <code>main</code> branch.</li>
      <li>Choose a machine with <strong>at least 4 cores</strong>. An <strong>8-core machine</strong> is preferred for the distributed application, local infrastructure emulators, builds, and tests.</li>
      <li>Create the Codespace and wait until the dev container finishes starting and the terminal is available. Do not run workshop commands while the container is still configuring.</li>
    </ol>
    <figure>
      <img
        src={`${base}images/workshop-setup/codespace-menu.png`}
        alt="The repository Code menu open on the Codespaces tab with New with options highlighted."
      />
      <figcaption>Open the Codespaces tab in your own repository and choose <strong>New with options…</strong>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <img
        src={`${base}images/workshop-setup/codespace-machine-options.png`}
        alt="GitHub Codespaces creation page for a workshop repository showing the main branch, dev container configuration, and 8-core machine option."
      />
      <figcaption>Select your repository's <code>main</code> branch, the workshop dev container, and a machine with at least four cores.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="verify-environment">
    <h2 id="verify-environment">Verify the environment</h2>
    <p>In the Codespace terminal, run each command and confirm it returns version information:</p>
    <pre><code class="language-bash">aspire --version
dotnet --version
dotnet --list-sdks
gh --version
copilot --version
gh stack --version</code></pre>
    <p>
      The prepared environment is expected to include the Aspire CLI, a .NET SDK,
      GitHub CLI, GitHub Copilot CLI, and the <code>gh stack</code> extension. If a
      command is missing or fails, stop and fix the Codespace setup before continuing;
      do not substitute a local installation.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <img
        src={`${base}images/workshop-setup/toolchain-checks.png`}
        alt="Codespace terminal showing the required Aspire, .NET, GitHub CLI, Copilot CLI, and gh stack version checks."
      />
      <figcaption>All six commands should return version information in the Codespace terminal.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="start-application">
    <h2 id="start-application">Start the application</h2>
    <p>From the repository root in the Codespace terminal, start the Aspire application:</p>
    <pre><code class="language-bash">aspire start</code></pre>
    <p>
      Aspire prints a Dashboard login URL containing a secret in its
      <code>t</code> query parameter. Copy that secret locally. It is required if
      you open the Dashboard through a forwarded port. Treat it as a credential:
      do not commit it, paste it into an issue, or share it in chat.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <img
        src={`${base}images/workshop-setup/aspire-start.png`}
        alt="Codespace terminal showing the Aspire CLI banner and startup output. The dashboard credential is intentionally not shown."
      />
      <figcaption>The Aspire CLI starts from the repository root. Keep the Dashboard login secret local.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="confirm-health">
    <h2 id="confirm-health">Confirm application health</h2>
    <p>
      Wait for the distributed resources to start, then run:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-bash">aspire describe</code></pre>
    <p>
      Continue when the required application services and infrastructure resources
      report <strong>Running</strong> and <strong>Healthy</strong>. The interactive
      <code>supporthub-cli</code> resource can remain <strong>NotStarted</strong>
      until you intentionally launch it.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <img
        src={`${base}images/workshop-setup/aspire-describe-health.png`}
        alt="Codespace terminal output from aspire describe, listing SupportHub resources with Running state and Healthy health."
      />
      <figcaption>Use <code>aspire describe</code> to wait for the expected resources to become healthy.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="open-dashboard">
    <h2 id="open-dashboard">Open the Aspire Dashboard</h2>
    <p>
      Alternatively, open the Codespaces <strong>Ports</strong> panel, locate
      <strong>aspire-dashboard-https (17119)</strong>, and open its forwarded address.
      When the Dashboard asks you to sign in, paste the secret you copied from the
      <code>aspire start</code> output.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <img
        src={`${base}images/workshop-setup/dashboard-port.png`}
        alt="Codespaces Ports panel with the aspire-dashboard-https port 17119 highlighted and its forwarded address available to open."
      />
      <figcaption>Open the forwarded address for <code>aspire-dashboard-https (17119)</code>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      A successful dashboard shows the SupportHub resources and their current
      states. Use it throughout the workshop to inspect resource health, logs,
      traces, and endpoints.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <img
        src={`${base}images/workshop-setup/dashboard-resources.png`}
        alt="Aspire Dashboard Resources view showing the SupportHub distributed application with its services and infrastructure running."
      />
      <figcaption>A healthy SupportHub run in the Aspire Dashboard.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="open-webfrontend">
    <h2 id="open-webfrontend">Open the Blazor frontend</h2>
    <p>
      In the Codespaces <strong>Ports</strong> panel, locate
      <strong>webfrontend-https (7274)</strong> and open its forwarded address.
      This confirms that the Blazor frontend is reachable through Aspire and
      Codespaces port forwarding.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <img
        src={`${base}images/workshop-setup/webfrontend-port.png`}
        alt="Codespaces Ports panel with webfrontend-https port 7274 highlighted and its forwarded address available to open."
      />
      <figcaption>Open the forwarded address for <code>webfrontend-https (7274)</code>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      A successful navigation displays the default SupportHub Blazor page with
      the Home and Counter navigation items. The workshop feature steps replace
      this baseline presentation incrementally.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <img
        src={`${base}images/workshop-setup/webfrontend-home.png`}
        alt="The default SupportHub Blazor home page showing a Hello, world welcome message and Home and Counter navigation."
      />
      <figcaption>The default Blazor frontend confirms the prepared application is reachable.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="inspect-reference-stack">
    <h2 id="inspect-reference-stack">Inspect the completed stacked PRs</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="https://github.com/sinannar/agentic-engineering-experiment">agentic-engineering-experiment</a>
      repository is the completed reference. Its stacked pull requests are
      intentionally still open so you can inspect their order and parent relationships.
    </p>
    <p>
      Open <a href="https://github.com/sinannar/agentic-engineering-experiment/pulls">its pull requests</a>
      and use GitHub's stack indicator to inspect the sequence. The reference stack
      starts with <code>feature/customers</code> targeting <code>main</code>, continues
      through <code>feature/tickets</code>, <code>feature/attachments</code>,
      <code>feature/activity</code>, <code>feature/sla</code>,
      <code>feature/dashboard</code>, and <code>feature/ui-ux</code>, with a
      <code>devcontainer</code> pull request on top.
    </p>
    <div class="setup-stack-images">
      <figure>
        <img
          src={`${base}images/workshop-setup/stack-picker.png`}
          alt="GitHub stack picker showing the devcontainer pull request above the UI/UX, dashboard, SLA, activity, attachments, tickets, and customers pull requests."
        />
        <figcaption>The stack picker displays the parent-to-child pull request order.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img
          src={`${base}images/workshop-setup/stack-merge.png`}
          alt="GitHub stack merge panel showing the ordered pull requests and their ready status."
        />
        <figcaption>The merge panel shows the ordered stack and its readiness.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img
          src={`${base}images/workshop-setup/stack-indicators.png`}
          alt="GitHub pull request list showing the stack position indicator next to every open reference pull request."
        />
        <figcaption>Stack indicators show every pull request's position in the sequence.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <p>
      Use the reference to understand the stack only. Do not merge, close, edit, or
      push to its pull requests.       Return to your workshop repository and continue to Customer Management.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="final-goal">
    <h2 id="final-goal">Final goal</h2>
    <p>
      By the end of the seven feature steps, your workshop repository should be a working
      SupportHub ticketing system: a focused operations dashboard, a filterable
      ticket queue, and customer records that support the work in that queue.
    </p>
    <p>
      The screens below show the intended end state. Customer and ticket record
      data has been redacted; use your own test data while completing the workshop.
    </p>
    <div class="setup-final-goal-images">
      <figure>
        <img
          src={`${base}images/workshop-setup/final-dashboard.png`}
          alt="Completed SupportHub overview dashboard showing open ticket, SLA, status, and priority summaries."
        />
        <figcaption>Operations overview with ticket and SLA summaries.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img
          src={`${base}images/workshop-setup/final-tickets.png`}
          alt="Completed SupportHub ticket queue with filtering controls. Ticket and customer record details are redacted."
        />
        <figcaption>Filterable ticket queue. Record details are redacted.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img
          src={`${base}images/workshop-setup/final-customers.png`}
          alt="Completed SupportHub customer directory. Customer record details are redacted."
        />
        <figcaption>Customer directory. Record details are redacted.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>
